 To apply a Gaussian filter before the Sobel edge detection algorithm in MATLAB, you can use the `imgaussfilt` function. This function applies a Gaussian filter to the input image. Here's how you can modify your code to include the Gaussian filter:
```MATLAB
% Read input image and convert to grayscale
input_image = imread('image.jpg');
gray_image = rgb2gray(input_image);
% Apply Gaussian filter to reduce noise
filtered_image = imgaussfilt(gray_image, 3); % Standard deviation of 3 pixels
% Define Sobel operator in x and y directions
sobel_x = [-1 0 1; -2 0 2; -1 0 1];
sobel_y = [-1 -2 -1; 0 0 0; 1 2 1];
% Apply Sobel operator in x direction to filtered image
edge_image_x = conv2(double(filtered_image), sobel_x, 'same');
% Apply Sobel operator in y direction to filtered image
edge_image_y = conv2(double(filtered_image), sobel_y, 'same');
% Combine edges in both directions
edge_image = sqrt(edge_image_x.^2 + edge_image_y.^2);
% Display original image, filtered image, and edge image
subplot(1,3,1), imshow(input_image), title('Original Image');
subplot(1,3,2), imshow(filtered_image), title('Filtered Image');
subplot(1,3,3), imshow(edge_image), title('Edge Image');
```
In this modified code, the `imgaussfilt` function is used to apply a Gaussian filter to the grayscale image with a standard deviation of 3 pixels. The filtered image is then used as the input for the Sobel edge detection algorithm. This helps reduce the noise in the image and improves the accuracy of the edge detection results.

